knowledge-base/log/2026/09/W38/_archive.md:1260:**The browser-clock defect, observed rather than inferred.** The Day brief showed `Afternoon Shift · 13:00 → 21:00 · 27% complete · 5h 51m left` while its own "Updated" read `04:40`. The browser was in Asia/Calcutta at 15:10; Panama was at 04:40. 27% of a 13:00–21:00 shift is 15:09 — legacy was reporting a shift that had not started in the operation's own timezone as a quarter over. It also confirms legacy's window is a real rostered slot with a future end, where ours is the trailing `[now − 8h, now]` that made a progress bar meaningless (decision 5, card 7.68).
knowledge-base/log/2026/09/W38/_archive.md:1268:**A design error found by implementing it.** Decision 5 as signed off said Canvas would render the plane's shift progress. The plane has none and cannot: `resolveWindow` builds `[now − shiftHours, now]` (`shiftHandoverBrief.ts:141-148`), a TRAILING window that is 100% elapsed by construction, so "% complete" has no meaning over it. Legacy's figures come from a hardcoded Morning/Afternoon/Night roster its own spec flags as needing "a code change, not configuration" per airline — and are computed from the BROWSER's clock, so two controllers already disagree about the same shift. Rendering a progress bar would have meant inventing a tenant shift schedule. The decision was rewritten mid-build: the brief states the window it covers and claims nothing about how far through a shift anyone is. Card 7.68.
knowledge-base/log/2026/09/W38/_archive.md:1284:**The Day brief renders the plane's nine sections with their statuses, not legacy's six tiles** (ruling BE): a tile shows a number, and a number cannot distinguish NONE from THE SOURCE FAILED, which is exactly what this plane's `ok`/`failed`/`absent_by_config` model exists to keep apart. Legacy's Crew-changes and Disruptions tiles have no section at all (cards 7.64, 7.65). The finished `exportShiftHandoverBrief`, which nothing consumes today, is wired with the byte-equality test its own design of record names. One legacy bug is deliberately not ported: shift progress is computed from the BROWSER's clock (`localHour`/`localMin`), so two controllers on different machines see different progress for the same shift; our plane resolves the window from tenant config and an injected clock.
