knowledge-base/log/2026/10/W41/2026-10-06-launchpad-connection-inbound-arrival.md:4:- **What:** The `turnaroundRisks` plane row gains `inboundArrival`, `inboundArrivalBasis` (`block_on`, `estimate`, `scheduled`) and the two floors it judged against (`turnaround.warningMins`, `turnaround.criticalMins`), all additive. `ConnectionTable` shows an Inbound arrival column that names the basis. `regradeConnection(row, now)` in `launchpad/horizonModel.ts` takes the minutes past an unlanded inbound's arrival off the buffer and escalates the grade; `clockedRead` applies it after dropping a departed outbound. The floors ride on the row because `requiredMins` is the critical floor on a critical row and cannot re-grade it, so the function needs no thresholds argument. The BFF passes the snapshot through unchanged.
knowledge-base/log/2026/10/W41/2026-10-06-launchpad-connection-inbound-arrival.md:5:- **Limits, by design:** only breached turns are on the plane, so a scrub can escalate or drop a row and cannot surface a connection that was compliant at fetch time. The clock moves forward only; a landed inbound keeps its recorded gap. The row does not carry the outbound's effective departure, so a delayed outbound is not re-timed.
