tools/canonical-model/reviews/0.10/critic-0.10-lens2.md:60:| 10-M8 | DutyPeriod history derivation storms at the end of the backfill and never runs for a crew with a standing exception | MAJOR | "History items are not written while the crew's history is uncovered, and one per crew is enqueued when it becomes covered" (`cet:1957`). Fleet history becomes covered at one moment, the end of the ≈ 92 h backfill, so about 9k crews (P2's population) each enqueue a year of legs at once. They share the `derive` kind and an unnamed worker with live items, whose warning is 600 s (`cet:4924`), so live DutyPeriods (the source of `absence.affectedDutyRefs`) lag behind the storm. Nothing names what detects "becomes covered". Under ruling 0.10-4, a crew with one unresolved ledger entry is never covered, so it never derives history. The history clock "after the crew's history is covered" never starts, so the gap is silent. | Run history derivation on its own worker, paced at N crews a minute, below live. Enqueue per crew-range as its fleet days cover, deriving around exception days, with the adjacent duties marked partial. Add a gauge, `crews_history_covered_not_derived`, with an age rule. |
tools/canonical-model/reviews/0.10/dba-0.10.md:48:| 10 | **The reset carry is sound, but five details are missing.** (a) Nothing stops a still-running writer from advancing the cursor between the read and the TRUNCATE. (b) The predicate (which feed, `kind = 'cursor'`, and whether the lag-reset replay cursor goes too) is not stated. (c) Without the GUC the read silently returns 0 rows under FORCE RLS. `resetData.ts:20` sets the GUC, but nothing reports what was carried. (d) `resetDataPlan.ts:31` gives the opposite rationale ("a kept cursor would resume past rows that are gone"). (e) For a rewound source (an AIMS restore, a re-dated synthetic corpus) the carried anchor relies on `checkLagRestoreDiscontinuity` (`domains/airline/crew/adapters/src/sync/rosterLinkedCoverageRotation.ts:301`). Also, `absence_signals_replayed_at_reset` needs a stored "this was a reset" marker, because `resetData` is a one-off with no metrics. | Low | Verified in the container: no GUC → 0 cursor rows visible; with the GUC, LOCK, TEMP, TRUNCATE and INSERT carried the row with `cursor_state` and `rev` intact. | (a) `LOCK TABLE stratum.sync_feed_items IN ACCESS EXCLUSIVE MODE` before the read, under the existing 10 s `lock_timeout`. (b) Name the predicate. (c) Add `carried: Record<string, number>` to `ResetResult` and the log line. (d) Rewrite the comment. (e) Add a red test, "reset + rewound source", and have the carry stamp a `carriedAtReset` field into `cursor_state`, which `cursor_state_object` allows, for the counter to read. Pre-existing, for the record: truncating `budget` lets the reseed spend a second full hourly ceiling against AIMS in the reset's clock hour. Consider carrying the current hour's budget row. |
